Suppose the system loses memory of its detailed position on a microscopic correlation or mixing time $\tau_{\rm mix}$. A rare region of probability $P(A)$ is then encountered on the scale $\tau_{\rm rec}
 \sim \frac{\tau_{\rm mix}}{P(A)}
 \sim \tau_{\rm mix}e^{\Delta S}.$ For the half-room condition, $\tau_{\rm rec}\sim\tau_{\rm mix}2^N.$ The lecture uses the deliberately rough room-sized estimate $N\sim10^{30}$, giving the memorable scale $\tau_{\rm rec}/\tau_{\rm mix}\sim2^{10^{30}}$. At ordinary atmospheric density a room is closer to $10^{27}$ molecules, but replacing $10^{30}$ by $10^{27}$ changes none of the conclusion: the recurrence time remains inconceivably larger than the age of the universe.

